  <section>
    <h2>The problem</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Office work has not changed much. The number of channels it runs through has.</p>
    <p>
      I asked five experts how work in an office changed with digitalisation. The information people exchange is
      largely the same as before. What changed are the channels: notice board, letter, phone and memo became invitation,
      chat, email, comment and voice message, and almost all of them now arrive on one screen.
    </p>
    <p>
      The thesis asked how a connected system could take a physical form, and whether working with it and its data
      would become richer that way. How work is spread across people and channels is still the question I work on at
      Levelwork.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <Image
        src={channels}
        alt="Sketch of the interview result: before digitalisation a person with notice board, letter, phone, note and memo; after, a person in front of a screen with invitation, chat, email, phone, note, appointment, comment and voice message"
        widths={widths}
        sizes="(max-width: 900px) 100vw, 75vw"
        loading="lazy"
      />
      <figcaption>What the interviews showed: the same information, many more channels, one screen.</figcaption>
    </figure>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What I did</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Literature, interviews, a scenario for 2045, then objects you can hold.</p>
    <p>
      After the literature and the five expert interviews I wrote a scenario for the office of 2045 and used speculative
      design to explore it: user stories with emotion curves, sketches, and models of the objects that I laser-cut and built by hand. In this
      office an AI takes over the organisation, and each kind of communication gets its own object. There is no app and
      no website.
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>conblocks</strong> plan the day in your hand: the AI hands out a block for each appointment.</li>
      <li><strong>connote</strong> is the notebook that carries the blocks, with space for notes.</li>
      <li><strong>concup</strong> is a cup that tells colleagues in other places that you are open for a chat.</li>
      <li><strong>condesk</strong> is a booth for working together across a distance.</li>
    </ul>
    <div class="c-pair">
      <figure>
        <Image
          src={sketchbook}
          alt="Sketchbook spread in pencil: a project plan as Tetris, the forerunner of the conblocks, notes on critical design and design fiction, and a coloured ring of a working day"
          widths={widths}
