      </figure>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Decisions</h2>
    <p class="c-lead">Add to the screen, don’t replace it.</p>
    <p>
      <strong>Data based interaction.</strong> Today input and output meet on a screen. The model I proposed puts a
      tangible representation next to the screen for the things that matter in the room, and keeps the screen for the
      rest. Conactive does not ban screens; it gives the most frequent kinds of contact a body.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>One object per kind of contact.</strong> Planning the day, an informal chat and working together across a
      distance need different signals. A cup in your hand says “talk to me” more clearly than a status in a chat window.
    </p>
    <figure class="c-wide">
      <ScrubFigure kind="conactive-channels" ratio="1200 / 640" narrow="600 / 900" />
      <figcaption>
        Five channels became eight on one screen. Conactive gives appointments, notes and the “open for a chat” signal an
        object of their own and leaves the rest on the screen.
      </figcaption>
    </figure>
    <figure class="c-wide">
